00:00:05.269 --> 00:00:07.590 align:start position:0%
hello everybody my name is paul scott  and i'm a research geneticist in the  usda ars and together with my co-host dr  thomas luberstedt from the iowa state  university department of agronomy i'm  pleased to invite you to the national  association of plant breeders meeting in  2022 which will be held  august 8th to 11th  in ames iowa  the most exciting thing about this  meeting for us is that we are  tentatively planning to hold it in  person  of course you know iowa is centrally  located  and it's closer than you think  there are more than 30 direct flights to  des moines international airport  the venue of the conference will be the  sheman building which is the conference  center  adjacent to the campus of iowa state  university  ames is a small town and it's easy to  get around there's  good public transportation and we'll be  running a shuttle from the conference  hotel to the sheman building but if you  prefer to stretch your legs it's a 37  minute walk  that goes by the scenic  reiman gardens and the jack tri stadium  we're still finalizing our  pre-conference tours but  there are many opportunities in the ames  area and i'm sure you'll be pleased with  what we come up with  the theme of the meeting is past present  and future of plant breeding  the past is represented by a celebration  of the 100th anniversary of  the federal state corn breeding program  in iowa  the present is shown  in in this picture  of the plant breeding faculty and  students at iowa state university many  of these people are helping to organize  the meeting  and of course we don't know what the  future of plant breeding holds but if  the activity at iowa state university is  any indication  it will involve  more interaction with engineers to  design new processes and technology to  increase the efficiency of plant  breeding  anybody who comes to iowa expects to see  corn and soybean breeding and you won't  be disappointed  but we think you'll be surprised at what  corn and soybean breeding looks like  today  and at the diversity of crops that are  bred in the ames area including cover  crops sorghum mung bean sunflower and  even marigold  you'll have an opportunity to tour the  north central regional plant  introduction station which is home of a  maze germplasm collection with 36 000  accessions as well as germplasm  collections for sunflower flax cucurbitz  carrots amaranth and many other crops  you'll have a chance to tour the iowa  state university agronomy farm including  the doubled haploid facility for maize  see you in ames
 

